<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page3-poker" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>One-Card Two-Round Poker</title>

  <p>
    Time for a bigger game. Poker is a <m>3 \times 3</m> game once we clean it
    up, but the method is the same one we used for Tennis.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-4-setup">
    <title>Section 5.4, The Setup</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 5.4 up through <q>If Player 1 is dealt the low
      card, he has the same three options.</q></alert> Stop there.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <exercise xml:id="check-poker-nine-strategies">
    <statement>
      <p>
        How many pure strategies does Player 1 have?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><m>9</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. A strategy picks one of <m>3</m> options for the high card
            and one of <m>3</m> for the low card, so <m>3 \times 3 = 9</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>6</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            A strategy is a <em>pair</em> of choices, one for each card, so we
            multiply. Think of outfits: <m>3</m> shirts and <m>3</m> pants
            make <m>9</m> outfits, not <m>6</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>3</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            bb, bf, and f are options for <em>one</em> card. A strategy has to
            say what Player 1 does with both cards.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    When you read on, the book throws out every strategy that doesn't bet at
    every opportunity with the high card. That leaves three strategies, and
    the book names each one by its low card option only:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>bb</alert>: bb if high, bb if low. Always bet, whatever the
          card.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>bf</alert>: bb if high, bf if low. Always bet at first. With
          the low card, fold if Player 2 bets back.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>f</alert>: bb if high, f if low. Fold right away with the low
          card.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-4-solving">
    <title>Section 5.4, Finding the Equilibrium</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Keep reading through <m>(q_1, q_2, q_3) = \left(\frac{8}{15},
      \frac{2}{15}, \frac{1}{3}\right)</m>.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      A few things to watch for:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            In the high card table, all three rows are the same. That makes
            sense now: all three strategies play bb with the high card.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The payoff matrix shows up twice. The second copy is the same
            matrix with the probabilities in the margins, just like Tennis.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The book skips the cases with two active strategies and says it
            will come back to them in Section 5.5. We're skipping 5.5, so just
            know those cases don't give any more equilibria.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <exercise xml:id="check-poker-why-equal">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Why does the book set
        <m>\pi_2(\sigma_1, bb) = \pi_2(\sigma_1, bf) = \pi_2(\sigma_1, f)</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Condition (1) of Theorem 5.2. All of Player 2's strategies are
            active, so they have to earn him the same payoff against
            <m>\sigma_1</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Same idea as Tennis, just with three strategies instead
            of two.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Condition (2) of Theorem 5.2.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Condition (2) compares active strategies to inactive ones, and
            it's an inequality. Here every strategy is active, and we want
            equalities.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Player 1 is mixing, so Player 1 must be indifferent.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Wrong player. These are Player 2's payoffs, so this is about
            Player 2 being indifferent among <em>his</em> strategies.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-poker-sum-one">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Where does the equation <m>p_1 + p_2 + p_3 = 1</m> come from?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The <m>p_i</m> are the probabilities of Player 1's three
            strategies, and he has to play one of them.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Probabilities always add up to <m>1</m>. The two
            indifference equations aren't enough to pin down three unknowns,
            so we need this one too.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It's a third equation from Player 2 being indifferent.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Setting three things equal only gives two independent equations.
            This third one has nothing to do with payoffs. It's just a fact
            about probabilities.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It comes from the game being zero-sum.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Zero-sum is about the players' <em>payoffs</em> adding to
            <m>0</m>. This equation is about Player 1's probabilities.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    So we found <m>(p_1, p_2, p_3) = \left(\frac{1}{5}, \frac{2}{5},
    \frac{2}{5}\right)</m>. Before reading on, let's think about what this
    actually means.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-poker-interpret-p2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What does <m>p_2 = \frac{2}{5}</m> mean?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\frac{2}{5}</m> of the time, Player 1 uses bf: bb if high, bf
            if low.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Remember bf is a full strategy. With the high card he
            still bets at every opportunity.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            With the high card, Player 1 bets and then folds
            <m>\frac{2}{5}</m> of the time.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            He never folds with the high card! The name bf only describes
            what he does with the low card.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Player 2 bets back and then folds <m>\frac{2}{5}</m> of the time.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The <m>p</m>'s belong to Player 1. Player 2's probabilities are
            the <m>q</m>'s.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-poker-expected-payoff">
    <statement>
      <p>
        How would you compute Player 1's expected payoff
        <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)</m>? (Don't actually compute it.)
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Add up all nine cells, each one's Player 1 payoff weighted by
            <m>p_i q_j</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. That's equation (5.2), same as Tennis but with nine cells
            instead of four. Since this is an equilibrium, Theorem 5.3 also
            gives a shortcut. You'll see both in the reading.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Add up the three diagonal cells, where both players use the
            strategy with the same name.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Every cell can happen, since each player picks his strategy
            independently. Plus the names mean different things for the two
            players, so the diagonal isn't special.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Average the nine payoffs.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That treats every cell as equally likely. Each cell happens with
            probability <m>p_i q_j</m>, so weight by that instead.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-4-rest">
    <title>Section 5.4, The Rest</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Finish reading Section 5.4.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      The rest is interpreting the results, including the two things you just
      reasoned out: what the probabilities mean as plans, and how to compute
      the expected payoff. Hint for the plans: with the low card, Player 1
      bets at first under both bb and bf, so he bets with probability
      <m>p_1 + p_2 = \frac{3}{5}</m>.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-5-4" iframe="question-form.html?section=5.4" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
